---
// The docs home: what coding.sh does for the reader, then one card per way to a
// first trace. The H1 and the lede come from nav.ts. No status inventory, source
// paths or other products here (the Roadmap and Compare pages hold those).
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Cards from "../../components/docs/Cards.astro";
import DocShot from "../../components/docs/DocShot.astro";
import { diagram } from "../../components/docs/markdown";
import { ISSUES_URL } from "../../data/site";

const start = [
  {
    href: "/docs/quickstart#your-agent",
    title: "Your own agent",
    body: "TypeScript or Python. Wrap your OpenAI or Anthropic client, run your agent, open the run.",
  },
  {
    href: "/docs/quickstart#coding-agent",
    title: "A coding agent",
    body: "Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and others. One install line adds hooks. Your next session is recorded, no code changes.",
  },
  {
    href: "/docs/evals#first-eval",
    title: "Your first eval",
    body: "Write three cases, run your agent on each one and grade what it prints. No recorded runs needed.",
  },
  {
    href: "/docs/how-to/self-host-team",
    title: "A team",
    body: "Run one container, connect each machine with `coding.sh init --remote`, and open the team viewer.",
  },
];

const tasks = [
  { href: "/docs/explore", title: "See what a run did", body: "Each step in order: prompts, model calls, tool calls with their output, file diffs and subagents." },
  { href: "/docs/cost", title: "Track cost", body: "Tokens and cost per session, agent, model, repo and day." },
  { href: "/docs/how-to/session-to-eval", title: "Turn failures into evals", body: "Keep a run that went wrong as a test case, so the next change is checked against it." },
  { href: "/docs/experiments", title: "Compare two versions", body: "Run both on the same cases and see what got better, what broke, and whether it is noise." },
  { href: "/docs/how-to/test-agents-md-change", title: "Test an AGENTS.md change", body: "Replay the moments your agent went wrong with and without your edit, and get a verdict." },
  { href: "/docs/mcp", title: "Let your agent read its runs", body: "Ask your agent what failed last time. It reads its own history over MCP." },
];

const howItWorks = diagram(
  "how-it-works",
  "Your agent, through the SDK or OpenTelemetry and coding.sh serve, and coding agents, through hooks, send their runs to ~/.coding.sh on your disk, with secrets removed first. The viewer, the CLI, your agent over MCP, and evals and experiments all read from there. A team server is optional.",
);
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    Open a run in the local viewer to see what happened, step by step. Turn the runs that went wrong into evals, and rerun them after you
    change a prompt, a model or AGENTS.md.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-trace">Start here</h2>
  <p>Pick what you have. Each path takes a few minutes.</p>
  <Cards cols={2} cards={start} />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Already on a framework? The OpenAI Agents SDK, LangGraph, the Vercel AI SDK, the Claude Agent SDK and any OpenTelemetry exporter can
      send traces to coding.sh. See <a href="/docs/otel">Frameworks (OpenTelemetry)</a>. Deploying an app? See
      <a href="/docs/production">Production apps</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <DocShot
    name="docs/sdk-session"
    bar="coding.sh serve"
    alt="The coding.sh viewer showing one coding-sh-triage run titled Viewer search misses tool output: the gpt-5-mini call from OpenAI selected, with 34 input and 195 output tokens, its duration and cost, and the answer bug, viewer."
    caption="One run of your own agent in the viewer: the model call, its tokens and cost, and the answer."
  />

  <h2 id="what">What you can do with it</h2>
  <Cards cols={2} cards={tasks} />

  <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
  <Fragment set:html={howItWorks} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your agent sends its runs with the SDK or OpenTelemetry to <code>coding.sh serve</code>, a local server you start once.
    </li>
    <li>A coding agent is recorded by a hook that writes one line to a local file and exits, so it adds only milliseconds to each step.</li>
    <li>coding.sh removes secrets, then stores each run as a session in <code>~/.coding.sh</code> on your machine.</li>
    <li>
      You read sessions in the terminal, in the viewer (<code>coding.sh ui</code>), or through your agent over MCP. Evals and experiments run
      on the same sessions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your sessions stay on your machine unless you connect a team server or a sink. The only request coding.sh makes on its own is a daily
      check for a new release, which <code>CODING_SH_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> turns off.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    coding.sh is new and changes fast. Commands and file formats can still change between releases. The <a href="/docs/roadmap">Roadmap</a>
    lists what is still missing. The <a href="/changelog">Changelog</a> lists what changed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="help">Get help</h2>
  <p>
    Run <code>coding.sh doctor</code> to check your setup. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> covers sessions that don't show
    up. Bugs and questions go to <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub issues</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
